WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.167
-Our next guest is
the National Book Award

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:05.200
winning author of her
critically acclaimed novels

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.834
"Salvage the Bones"
and "Sing, Unburied, Sing."

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:10.834
Her fantastic new novel,
"Let Us Descend,"

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:12.234
was published today.

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:14.567
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jesmyn Ward.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:22.801
♪♪

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:29.267
♪♪

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.501
Welcome back.
-It's good to be here.

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:33.601
-I'm so happy to have you.
I asked you backstage --

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.234
are you relieved
once a book is written?

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:37.400
And you were very honest,
and you said you'd be relieved

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.968
when the press is over.
-Yes.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:42.701
-Well, thank you very much
for getting through it with us.

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:45.367
This -- I know people have
told you this over the years,

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.868
and it's something
that I believe, as well.

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:49.767
Your characters stay with people

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.968
long after
they've read your books.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.267
Is that something --

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.901
Is that a compliment
that you enjoy hearing?

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:56.934
Is that something you're trying
to do when you're writing?

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.067
-Yes.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.200
Yes, because I think

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.868
that literature
and that stories

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:07.667
act in a way that...

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.501
other mediums don't, right?

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:14.601
I think they allow readers to
sink into a world with a person

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:18.734
and to feel like they're living
that reality with that person.

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:21.567
And I think that the people
that I write about,

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:23.400
the characters
that I write about,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.567
are worthy of
that kind of regard, so...

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:28.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:33.133
-I know -- When you were here
for your National Book Award

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:34.701
winning book,
"Sing, Unburied, Sing,"

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:35.934
that's 2017.

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:37.567
You'd started this book.
-Yes.

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.801
-And I know it was
a very difficult book to write,

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:42.601
and it's about
very difficult themes.

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:45.167
I also want to say
I found it very easy to read,

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.701
which is a credit
to the beautiful way you write.

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:49.601
But can you talk about
getting over

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:53.367
that hurdle of addressing
sort of the themes

00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:55.467
and the difficult histories
that you're addressing in this?

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:58.634
-Right. This...

00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:00.467
It was really difficult
to write this book

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:04.100
in part because
I'd never written about anyone

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.434
who didn't have a lot
of physical agency, right?

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:10.267
I mean, Annis is
the main character in here,

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:12.067
and she's very constrained

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:14.801
because she's
an enslaved person, right?

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.767
But in the writing of the book,

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:21.601
I began to discover that she has
other types of agency, right?

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:25.868
She has emotional agency.
She has imaginative agency.

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:28.000
She has the agency of memory.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.300
She also has a type
of spiritual agency.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.901
So I think that
all of these agencies combined

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:37.100
gave her a way
to move through the world

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.033
that was not confined, right.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:43.100
that was not constrained
by the system of slavery.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.434
And I think it made it --

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:47.834
Hopefully it will make it easier
for readers to read

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.968
because they can experience

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:53.534
all of those freedoms
and agencies with her.

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:55.033
-There is, you know,

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.367
obviously a great deal
of tragedy in this book,

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.801
a great deal of grief
and the processing of grief,

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:01.834
but it's also
a very hopeful book.

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:05.334
When you're writing it,
do you know these are all things

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:07.801
that you want to make sure
exist all at once?

00:03:07.801 --> 00:03:10.000
Like, there's no obviously
neat endings,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.667
and it's not like
one section is about this,

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:13.300
one section is about that.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.667
Is it always important for you

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.734
to have it sort of
under the same umbrella?

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:19.834
-I think so. I mean...

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.868
You know, like, she -- I --

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:26.868
Annis is not a victim.
And she's also --

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.501
Because a victim --

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:30.400
When a person
is portrayed as a victim,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.367
they're flattened, right?

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:34.167
They're just victorious,
and that's it.

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:39.467
But she's also not -- She's not
only a survivor either, right?

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:41.334
Because when someone
is only a survivor,

00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:43.801
then their experience
is also flattened, right?

00:03:43.801 --> 00:03:47.901
I wanted her to be
as complicated and complex

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:50.267
as I could possibly make her.

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:54.968
So she experiences friendship,
she experiences connection,

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:58.400
she has lovers, you know,
at the same time

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.367
that she also feels grief
and feels longing

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:06.100
and, you know, misses her
community and misses friends.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.634
So there's -- I want all of that
to exist at the same time

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:14.000
because I think that reflects
how complicated we all are.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.534
-When you write such a sort of
multi-dimensional character

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:21.667
like this that does have
this great wealth of humanity,

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:23.200
do you find when you're writing

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.734
the character is telling you
about themselves

00:04:25.734 --> 00:04:27.968
or do you find
that you're putting yourself

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:30.033
into the character?

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:33.567
-I think there's a little bit
of both happening, right?

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:36.701
Because whenever I write,

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:40.667
I always feel like the character
who is speaking

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:43.634
is sitting here
and telling me the story.

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:45.934
So in part I'm channeling them.

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:50.567
But at the same time,
I'm also, I think,

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:52.634
pulling on my own experience

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:58.133
to try to understand some of
what they are going through.

00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:02.067
And I definitely --
I think this book is --

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:05.167
as far as my novels go --
is the book that is most,

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:09.234
I think, informed
by my experience.

00:05:09.234 --> 00:05:10.934
-After a day where you feel

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:14.067
as though you've done
very good writing,

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:16.067
what do you do
at the end of it?

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:17.934
Like, when you finish --
when you like --

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.968
Do you write out longhand
or laptop? Is it...

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:21.267
-Laptop?
-Alright. Laptop.

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:24.067
You close the laptop.
You're feeling good.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.634
What's the next thing you do?

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:30.767
-Uh...I...

00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:33.601
I do several --
There are several different ways

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:36.367
that I waste time
and cut off my brain.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:38.734
-It's very -- I think that's
what we all want to hear,

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:41.467
is that you also waste time.
-Yes, yes.

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:44.601
-Sometimes when prolific writers
who have great skill come on,

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:46.267
we want to also know
that they will --

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:48.067
"Yeah. I look at my phone."

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:50.701
-Right, right, right.
-So thank you.

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:52.734
You're giving us great relief
by saying that, as well.

00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:54.634
-Good.
-Also I want to,

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:57.100
before we let you go,
point out if people missed it.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.634
This is very exciting.

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:01.868
This was just named
an Oprah Book Club pick.

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:04.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:06.567
And, uh...

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:09.601
And once again, I'm --
Nothing makes me happier

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:12.701
than when my tastes
are in line with Oprah.

00:06:12.701 --> 00:06:15.367
And I think we both
have excellent taste.

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:17.601
And it's really just
a wonderful, wonderful book.

00:06:17.601 --> 00:06:19.934
And it's so happy --
I'm so happy to have you back.

00:06:19.934 --> 00:06:21.801
Thank you so much, Jesmyn.
-Thank you.

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:24.868
-You guys, that's Jesmyn Ward.

00:06:24.868 --> 00:06:26.133
"Let Us Descend"
is available now

00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:27.801
wherever you buy your books,
but please support

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:29.100
local and independent
booksellers.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.968
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

